WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.570
In the spring of sixteen sixty-one, the half-Japanese son of a pirate arrived with four hundred junks and demanded an island from the richest company in Europe.

2
00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.580
The fortress was thought to be impregnable.

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:17.830
Nine months later, the Dutch lowered their flags and marched out of it in formation.

4
00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:21.640
Cannon fire was not the only thing that had beaten them.

5
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:28.490
A storm, a traitorous sergeant, and warnings ignored for forty years also played their part in that defeat.

6
00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:44.020
In sixteen twenty-four, two events took place at the same moment, and for a long time neither had anything to do with the other.

7
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:54.710
On the southwestern coast of Taiwan, the large island facing the southern coast of China, soldiers of the Dutch East India Company built their first wooden fortifications on a spit of sand.

8
00:00:54.710 --> 00:01:02.200
Over the years, that outpost grew into a fortress with stone walls and corner bastions, and it came to be called Fort Zeelandia.

9
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.130
That same year, in the Japanese port of Hirado, a baby was born.

10
00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.620
His mother was a Japanese woman from the Tagawa family.

11
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:18.270
His father was a Chinese merchant and pirate who would become one of the most powerful men on the seas of East Asia: Zheng Zhilong.

12
00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.240
The child was given the Japanese name Fukumatsu.

13
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:28.290
Thirty-seven years later, he would tear down the world the Dutch had built on that spit of sand.

14
00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.900
The life of Zheng Zhilong could fill a novel on its own.

15
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:41.190
As a young man he spent time in Portuguese-held Macau, was baptized a Christian, and worked for a while as an interpreter for the Dutch.

16
00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.720
Then he built a fleet of his own.

17
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:50.890
Soon no ship carrying silk, porcelain or silver could pass through the Taiwan Strait without paying him for protection.

18
00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:56.140
When the Ming dynasty realized it could not defeat him, it chose to buy him instead.

19
00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:02.350
Zheng Zhilong was made an admiral, and overnight the pirate became the state's guardian of the coast.

20
00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.800
The appointment also let him multiply his fortune many times over.

21
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:13.650
When Fukumatsu was seven years old, he left his mother behind in Japan and was brought to China to live with his father.

22
00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:20.500
There he was given a Chinese name, the finest tutors were hired for him, and he was schooled in the classical texts.

23
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.950
In time the young man entered the imperial academy in Nanjing.

24
00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:30.520
The pirate's son, in other words, was being raised to be a scholar and a servant of the state.

25
00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.890
History had other plans.

26
00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:41.500
In sixteen forty-four, the Ming capital of Beijing fell, first to a peasant rebellion and then to the Manchus sweeping down from the north.

27
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.470
The Manchus proclaimed a new dynasty: the Qing.

28
00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:51.040
In the south, princes of the Ming line took the throne one after another and tried to hold out.

29
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.770
One of them, who reigned as the Longwu Emperor, depended on the support of the Zheng family.

30
00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:04.260
He received the young man at court, was greatly impressed by him, and granted him Zhu, the surname of the imperial family.

31
00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:13.510
From then on, people addressed him by a title meaning "Lord of the Imperial Surname." In the dialect of southern China, that title sounded something like Kok-seng-ia.

32
00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:18.600
The Dutch wrote it down as Koxinga, and that is the name Europe came to know him by.

33
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.890
In Chinese sources, his proper name is Zheng Chenggong.

34
00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.860
His father, Zheng Zhilong, read the winds differently.

35
00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:32.430
In sixteen forty-six, taken in by the promises of the Qing, he surrendered to them.

36
00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:37.040
Meanwhile, Qing soldiers stormed the family stronghold at Anping.

37
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:44.370
Koxinga's mother, who had come over from Japan only a few years earlier, lost her life in that raid.

38
00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.700
Most accounts say she took her own life.

39
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:55.830
Soon afterward, Koxinga went to a Confucian temple, burned his scholar's robes there, and declared that he was now a soldier.

40
00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:59.960
He had turned his back on his father and bound himself to the Ming cause.

41
00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:05.370
Over the next twelve years, Koxinga became master of the southern Chinese coast.

42
00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:09.020
The islands of Xiamen and Kinmen were his bases.

43
00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:16.350
He commanded tens of thousands of soldiers and hundreds of ships, and he fed his army on the income of seaborne trade.

44
00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:22.720
In sixteen fifty-nine he made a bold move, sailing up the Yangtze River to lay siege to Nanjing.

45
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.210
The campaign ended in disaster.

46
00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:34.340
Qing cavalry scattered his army, and Koxinga fell back to the coast, having lost thousands of men and some of his best commanders.

47
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:45.030
The Qing, for their part, were preparing a ruthless strategy against him: they would drive the coastal population inland and empty the shoreline, cutting off his supplies.

48
00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:48.840
Koxinga needed new land across the sea.

49
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:52.970
It was at exactly this moment that a man arrived from Taiwan.

50
00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:58.540
He Bin was a Chinese go-between who had worked for the Dutch as an interpreter and tax collector.

51
00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:04.110
Cornered by charges of debt and corruption, he fled to Xiamen in sixteen fifty-nine.

52
00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:14.960
According to the accounts, he brought with him a map and a priceless piece of information: a sea channel that lay beyond the range of Zeelandia's guns, one the Dutch had dismissed because it was so shallow.

53
00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:21.650
He Bin told Koxinga that the island was fertile, that the Dutch were few in number, and that the door stood open.

54
00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.860
The Dutch had heard the rumors.

55
00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:36.630
The governor of Taiwan, Frederick Coyett, was a seasoned Company official born in Sweden, and for a long time he had been writing letters of warning to Batavia, the Company's Asian headquarters in what is today Jakarta.

56
00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:41.880
Among the Chinese farmers on the island, there was talk that an attack was coming soon.

57
00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.370
Some of them were abandoning their fields and fleeing.

58
00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:53.980
In sixteen sixty, Batavia finally sent a relief fleet: twelve ships and roughly six hundred soldiers under the command of Jan van der Laan.

59
00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:57.790
But van der Laan thought the danger had been exaggerated.

60
00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:04.320
His real goal was to attack Portuguese-held Macau, and waiting around in Taiwan struck him as a waste of time.

61
00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:13.730
He quarreled bitterly with Coyett, accused him of cowardice, and in February of sixteen sixty-one he sailed back to Batavia, taking most of his officers with him.

62
00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:20.420
Some of the soldiers stayed behind at the fortress, but the Company's most experienced men were gone.

63
00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:30.630
About two months later, on the morning of April thirtieth, sixteen sixty-one, the sentries on the ramparts of Zeelandia looked out into the mist and could not believe their eyes.

64
00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.800
The horizon was crowded with masts.

65
00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:38.090
Koxinga's fleet was made up of hundreds of junks.

66
00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:42.540
In the Dutch records, the figure climbs as high as about four hundred.

67
00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.710
The ships carried tens of thousands of soldiers.

68
00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:50.000
Most historians settle on a figure of around twenty-five thousand.

69
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.610
The main channel in front of the fortress lay under the mouths of the Dutch cannons.

70
00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.620
Koxinga did not go in there.

71
00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:02.710
Instead, he headed for a passage a few kilometers to the north, known as Lu'erhmen.

72
00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:08.120
This passage was so shallow that the Dutch were certain large ships could never get through it.

73
00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:13.530
But that morning, as local accounts insist, the sea was higher than usual.

74
00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:19.900
Chinese tradition attributes this to a prayer Koxinga offered to Mazu, the goddess who protects sailors.

75
00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:24.190
The Dutch records simply note that the water rose to an unusual level.

76
00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:32.160
Whichever explanation we choose, the outcome was the same: the junks slipped through the passage on the tide and entered the inner lagoon.

77
00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.250
The sea shield the Dutch had relied on for years had been breached in a single morning.

78
00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:42.180
Once inside the lagoon, the fleet split the Dutch positions in two.

79
00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.470
On one side stood Fort Zeelandia, on its sandy island.

80
00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:53.160
On the other, across the water on the mainland, at a place called Saccam, stood the smaller Fort Provintia.

81
00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:56.650
The water between them now belonged to Koxinga.

82
00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.660
Chinese soldiers landed on the mainland.

83
00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:08.430
The Chinese settlers of the surrounding area, farmers who had paid taxes to the Company for years, rushed out to watch them, and some came to help.

84
00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:12.080
They brought carts, oars, and pack animals.

85
00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:15.890
Within a few hours, thousands of soldiers were ashore.

86
00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:21.460
Coyett had a few warships, a few hundred musketeers, and one assumption.

87
00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:30.870
That assumption had been distilled from the Company's decades of experience in Asia: Chinese soldiers cannot stand up to firearms, and they scatter after a few volleys.

88
00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:35.000
The next day, he decided to put that assumption to the test.

89
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.850
The plan was a three-pronged counterattack.

90
00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:49.500
At sea, the ships would attack the junks in the lagoon; one detachment would try to reinforce Provintia; and another would march against the Chinese soldiers who had landed on the sandy island north of the fortress.

91
00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:53.310
At first, the naval action looked promising.

92
00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:59.200
The Dutch ships were far larger than the junks and carried far more guns.

93
00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.690
The opening volleys smashed many of the junks.

94
00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:05.860
But the Chinese sailors did not fall back.

95
00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:12.070
With their small vessels, they surrounded the Dutch, pulled up alongside them, and sent in fire ships.

96
00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:18.120
In the middle of this chaos, the largest Dutch warship, the Hector, suddenly exploded.

97
00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:24.170
Its powder magazine had probably been ignited either by its own guns or by fire hurled at close range.

98
00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.700
Most of the crew were killed.

99
00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.710
The remaining ships withdrew.

100
00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:38.640
One of them, a small yacht called the Maria, struggled against the wind for weeks to reach Batavia, and it was the first to bring news of the disaster there.

101
00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:44.370
When the detachment sent to Provintia neared the shore, it found a large Chinese force waiting.

102
00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:49.620
Only a small number of the soldiers managed to get into the fort; the rest turned back.

103
00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:55.030
And so, that day, nothing had been achieved, either at sea or on the mainland.

104
00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:59.320
That left the third prong: the sandy island to the north.

105
00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:02.490
That sandy island was called Baxemboy.

106
00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:08.060
It was a narrow strip of land stretching north of Zeelandia, nothing but dunes and scrub.

107
00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:13.950
Coyett sent about two hundred and forty soldiers there, led by Captain Thomas Pedel.

108
00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:18.400
Pedel was an aging officer who had spent many years in the colony.

109
00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:23.170
He had put down Chinese peasant revolts and clashed with the Chinese time and again.

110
00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:30.020
In his eyes, and in the eyes of many in the fortress, what they faced was not a dangerous enemy but a mere crowd.

111
00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:40.390
According to the defense Coyett later wrote, the Dutch truly believed that Chinese soldiers could not bear the smell of gunpowder, and that as soon as a few of them fell dead, the whole lot would run.

112
00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:44.520
Pedel's soldiers landed on the beach and advanced in neat ranks.

113
00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:48.170
Facing them stood several thousand Chinese soldiers.

114
00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:54.380
The Dutch fired in turn, just as they had drilled: first the front rank, then the ranks behind.

115
00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:57.710
The Chinese answered with arrows and muskets.

116
00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:01.040
The rout the Dutch had been expecting never came.

117
00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:03.890
The Chinese soldiers did not budge.

118
00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:08.660
Meanwhile, Koxinga's commanders had made a move the Dutch could not see.

119
00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:14.550
One unit had circled around behind the dunes and slipped onto the flank and rear of Pedel's column.

120
00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:18.520
Suddenly the Dutch were under pressure from the front and from behind.

121
00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:23.610
At that very moment, the most feared troops in the Chinese army stepped forward.

122
00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:29.660
These were heavy infantrymen clad in armor of iron scales, known in some sources as the iron men.

123
00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:39.550
They carried heavy, long-handled swords gripped with both hands, whose name meant "horse-cutting sword." They advanced as one body, with disciplined steps.

124
00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:42.880
The Dutch ranks broke apart.

125
00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:48.610
Soldiers threw down their muskets and ran for the water, and some flung themselves into the waves.

126
00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:51.140
Pedel died fighting.

127
00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:58.150
According to Dutch records, nearly half the unit, one hundred and eighteen men, lost their lives on that beach.

128
00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:02.280
The survivors barely made it back to the fortress by boat.

129
00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:10.090
What Coyett watched that day from the fortress bastions was the collapse of a legend that had carried the Company's entire confidence in Asia.

130
00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:19.020
The historian Tonio Andrade examines this clash as a moment that put to the test the assumption that the European musket was inherently superior in Asia.

131
00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:22.670
Koxinga's soldiers had fought the Qing for years.

132
00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:27.120
They were an experienced, well-trained, tightly disciplined army.

133
00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:30.450
The Dutch had taken this army for a crowd of peasants.

134
00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:35.060
After the defeat on the beach, Provintia could not hold out for long.

135
00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:40.310
The fort held only a handful of soldiers, with little water and little gunpowder.

136
00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:45.720
Koxinga surrounded them and let them know that if they surrendered, their lives would be spared.

137
00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:52.890
On the fourth of May, sixteen sixty-one, Provintia's commander, Jacobus Valentijn, surrendered the fort.

138
00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:56.540
Not a single Dutch flag now remained on the main island.

139
00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:02.750
The Dutch held only Zeelandia, on its sandy islet, and the small town beneath its walls.

140
00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:07.360
Koxinga now wanted to persuade Coyett to surrender without bloodshed.

141
00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:17.090
In his letters he wrote that the island had in fact belonged to his father, that the Dutch had only ever stayed there as guests, and that the time had come to return it to its rightful owner.

142
00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:24.420
As one of the envoys to carry these messages, he chose an extraordinary man: a Dutch minister named Antonius Hambroek.

143
00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:34.150
Hambroek had spent years as a missionary in the island's indigenous villages, and at the start of the war he had been taken captive by the Chinese, along with part of his family.

144
00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:37.000
Koxinga sent him to Zeelandia.

145
00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:42.410
His task was to deliver the surrender offer to Coyett and to persuade him to accept it.

146
00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:46.220
Hambroek entered the fortress and delivered the offer.

147
00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:50.670
Then he did the thing that would be retold in the Netherlands for generations.

148
00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:57.360
According to the accounts, he told the defenders not to surrender, that help was on its way, and that they must hold on.

149
00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:01.170
His daughters were inside the fortress too.

150
00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:10.900
Tradition says they begged him to stay, but Hambroek, believing that the other captives in Chinese hands would suffer if he did not return, walked back to Koxinga's camp.

151
00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:19.670
Months later, when rumors spread that the Dutch were inciting the indigenous villages against the Chinese, Hambroek was executed along with other Dutch captives.

152
00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:23.320
Hambroek was remembered in the Netherlands as a hero.

153
00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:30.650
In the eighteenth century his story was brought to the stage, and it became an epic of country and conscience taught in Dutch schools.

154
00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:34.140
The fortress, however, did not surrender.

155
00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:38.590
In the last week of May, Koxinga decided to show his strength.

156
00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:45.760
Under cover of night, gun emplacements were set up in the town at the foot of the fortress, and at dawn a heavy bombardment began.

157
00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.930
But Zeelandia was an advanced design.

158
00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:55.940
The projecting bastions at its corners were placed so that every point along the walls could be covered by flanking fire.

159
00:14:55.940 --> 00:15:02.150
The Dutch gunners answered the exposed Chinese batteries and inflicted terrible losses.

160
00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:06.920
When the firing stopped, the streets of the town were filled with corpses.

161
00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:11.690
Koxinga learned his lesson: this fortress could not be taken by assault.

162
00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:16.140
He encircled it and settled in to wait for hunger and time to do their work.

163
00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:19.310
The siege dragged on all summer.

164
00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:27.600
Hundreds of soldiers and civilians were trapped inside the fortress: Company officials, families, slaves, the wounded.

165
00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:31.410
Food was running low, and disease was spreading.

166
00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:36.180
The heat, the humidity and the foul water brought scurvy and dysentery.

167
00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:40.150
Outside, things were not easy for Koxinga's army either.

168
00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:49.720
An army this large had to be fed, and supplies from the Chinese coast had become harder to obtain because of the Qing policy of evacuating the coastline.

169
00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:57.050
The soldiers gathered rice from the island's indigenous villages and from Chinese farmers, and then began clearing fields.

170
00:15:57.050 --> 00:16:01.820
One side bore the weight of hunger, the other the weight of impatience.

171
00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:07.550
In August, sails appeared once more from Zeelandia's bastions.

172
00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:10.880
This time, those arriving were friends.

173
00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:16.610
On the news the Maria had carried to Batavia, the Company's leadership had sent a relief fleet.

174
00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:20.100
At its head was Jacob Caeuw.

175
00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:25.030
Under his command were about ten ships and some seven hundred soldiers.

176
00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:28.200
A festive mood swept through the fortress.

177
00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:32.650
But once again, the sea around Taiwan took Koxinga's side.

178
00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:36.140
As the fleet tried to anchor, a storm struck.

179
00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:40.750
The ships were driven out to sea and for weeks could not get near the fortress.

180
00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:46.480
One of the ships, the Urk, ran aground, and its crew fell into Chinese hands.

181
00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:56.850
In an instant, the relief had lost provisions, ammunition and, most important of all, intelligence: the captives told Koxinga how strong the fleet was.

182
00:16:56.850 --> 00:17:01.140
When the storm had passed, the Dutch planned a counterattack.

183
00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:10.230
On September sixteenth, sixteen sixty-one, ships and landing troops struck at the Chinese fleet in the lagoon and at the positions along the shore.

184
00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:14.040
The result was a repeat of Baxemboy.

185
00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:17.850
The Dutch ships could not maneuver in the narrow, shallow waters.

186
00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:25.020
The Chinese junks swarmed them in great numbers, several of the ships ran aground or were captured, and many soldiers were killed.

187
00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:30.590
Once again, the men on the fortress walls had watched a rout unfold before their eyes.

188
00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:34.720
That autumn, an unexpected player entered the war.

189
00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:39.010
Qing commanders on the Chinese mainland offered the Dutch an alliance.

190
00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:41.860
Their common enemy was Koxinga.

191
00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:47.590
Coyett saw this as an opportunity and decided to send a fleet to conduct the negotiations.

192
00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:50.920
The mission was given to Caeuw.

193
00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:55.050
Caeuw set sail in December with a handful of ships.

194
00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:58.220
But he never went to the Chinese coast.

195
00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:02.990
He turned his course south, to Siam, and from there on to Batavia.

196
00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:08.400
Only much later would the fortress learn that the man who had come to help them had abandoned them.

197
00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:12.530
That same December, betrayal came from inside the fortress as well.

198
00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:19.380
A sergeant of German origin named Hans Jurgen Radis slipped over the walls one night and crossed to Koxinga's camp.

199
00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:27.510
Radis told them that morale in the fortress had hit rock bottom, that the soldiers were being cut down by disease, and that the provisions were running out.

200
00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:31.160
But what mattered most was the military advice he offered.

201
00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:35.770
Right beside Zeelandia stood a sand dune that looked down on the fortress.

202
00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:41.180
On that rise, the Dutch had built a small fortification they called the Utrecht Redoubt.

203
00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:44.830
According to Radis, this was the key to the fortress.

204
00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:51.680
If Utrecht fell, the courtyard and the interior of the fortress could be brought under cannon fire from there.

205
00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:54.850
Koxinga took this advice seriously.

206
00:18:54.850 --> 00:19:00.100
For weeks, his men dug earthworks, trenches, and gun emplacements.

207
00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:06.150
The knowledge of Radis and the other European deserters was combined with the skill of the Chinese gunners.

208
00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:12.040
On the morning of January twenty-fifth, sixteen sixty-two, the bombardment began.

209
00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:19.050
According to Dutch records, some two thousand five hundred cannonballs were fired at the Utrecht Redoubt that day.

210
00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:23.020
The small fortification was shattered within the first hours.

211
00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:29.390
By evening, the Dutch realized they could not hold it, and before withdrawing, they blew the redoubt up.

212
00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:35.600
But the hill now belonged to Koxinga, and the inside of Zeelandia lay within sight of his guns.

213
00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:39.730
The war council in the fortress met over several days.

214
00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:46.420
Some officers wanted one final assault; others argued that further resistance would now mean nothing but more dead.

215
00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:51.830
After a siege of nearly nine months, the survivors were exhausted.

216
00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:54.360
The council decided to surrender.

217
00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:59.450
On February first, sixteen sixty-two, the treaty was signed.

218
00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:06.780
Koxinga was generous: he allowed the Dutch to march out of the fortress with their weapons and personal belongings, their flags flying.

219
00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:12.830
The goods in the Company's warehouse, the cannons, and the money, however, were left to Koxinga.

220
00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:18.240
A few days later, Coyett and the survivors boarded their ships and set out for Batavia.

221
00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:22.370
The Netherlands' thirty-eight years on Taiwan had come to an end.

222
00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:28.580
After raising his own flag over the ramparts of Zeelandia, Koxinga began to reorganize the island.

223
00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:33.510
He gave the fortress the name of his family's stronghold back home: Anping.

224
00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:41.960
He organized the island as Ming territory, settled his soldiers on the land and made them farmers, and established administrative districts.

225
00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:45.130
His eyes were already turning further south.

226
00:20:45.130 --> 00:20:50.700
Through an Italian priest, he sent a letter to the Spanish governor in Manila demanding tribute.

227
00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:55.470
It seemed he was contemplating an expedition to the Philippines.

228
00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:58.800
But this victory did not grant him a long life.

229
00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:08.530
While the siege was still underway, in the autumn of sixteen sixty-one, the Qing had executed his father, Zheng Zhilong, along with the other members of the family in Beijing.

230
00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:13.300
Soon afterward came news that the last Ming emperor had been captured in Burma.

231
00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:20.310
The dynasty for which Koxinga had fought had, for all practical purposes, ceased to exist.

232
00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:31.160
On top of this, a family scandal broke: his eldest son, Zheng Jing, who had remained in Xiamen, had had an affair with his younger brother's wet nurse, and a child had been born of it.

233
00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:35.930
In Koxinga's eyes, this was an unforgivable moral transgression.

234
00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:39.740
He ordered the execution of his son and of his son's mother.

235
00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:44.030
The commanders in Xiamen refused to carry out the order.

236
00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:47.520
His own officers would no longer obey him.

237
00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:51.330
Koxinga fell ill in June of sixteen sixty-two.

238
00:21:51.330 --> 00:21:54.340
It was most likely malaria.

239
00:21:54.340 --> 00:22:01.190
On June twenty-third, five months after the surrender of Zeelandia, he died at the age of thirty-seven.

240
00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:06.120
Contemporary accounts say that his final days were filled with rage and grief.

241
00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:12.330
The man the Dutch had seen as an invincible conqueror died watching his own family and his cause fall apart.

242
00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:15.180
And what became of the loser?

243
00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:20.270
When Frederick Coyett arrived in Batavia, he was not welcomed as a hero.

244
00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:27.280
The Company's leadership was looking for someone to blame for this great loss, and the easiest target was the governor himself.

245
00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:31.730
Coyett was arrested, tried, and sentenced to death.

246
00:22:31.730 --> 00:22:36.340
The sentence was later commuted to lifelong exile in the Banda Islands.

247
00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:39.510
He lived for years on a remote island.

248
00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:47.960
Thanks to petitions from his family and the intervention of influential friends in the Netherlands, he was released in sixteen seventy-four and returned to Europe.

249
00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:55.610
The year after, in sixteen seventy-five, a book appeared in Amsterdam, signed not with a name but with initials.

250
00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:59.420
Its Dutch title can be rendered as Neglected Formosa.

251
00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:05.150
Formosa was the name Portuguese sailors had given to Taiwan, and it meant beautiful island.

252
00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:09.280
Neglected Formosa was Coyett's defense of his own case.

253
00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:16.290
According to the book, what lost the colony was not the governor's cowardice, but the blindness of the administrators in Batavia.

254
00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:26.660
The warnings had not been taken seriously, van der Laan had packed up and left without good reason, Caeuw had abandoned the fortress, and help had always come too late and too little.

255
00:23:26.660 --> 00:23:30.470
The book was filled with letters, testimonies and documents.

256
00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:35.720
Rather than accounting for a defeat, it laid bare the inner workings of an institution.

257
00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:44.170
Even today, every historian who sets out to tell the story of the siege turns to this book, knowing full well that Coyett was anything but impartial.

258
00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:48.140
The state Koxinga founded outlived him by twenty-one years.

259
00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:56.590
Zheng Jing, who had escaped his father's order of execution, took over the governance of the island and carried on the resistance in the name of the Ming.

260
00:23:56.590 --> 00:24:00.720
But across the sea, the Qing were growing stronger.

261
00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:08.210
In sixteen eighty-three, the Qing navy defeated the Zheng fleet off the Penghu Islands, between Taiwan and the mainland.

262
00:24:08.210 --> 00:24:10.900
The commander of that navy was Shi Lang.

263
00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:15.670
Years earlier, Shi Lang had been one of Koxinga's officers.

264
00:24:15.670 --> 00:24:22.200
Because of a quarrel between them, his family had been killed on Koxinga's orders, and he had gone over to the Qing.

265
00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:30.490
Koxinga's grandson, Zheng Keshuang, surrendered, and for the first time, Taiwan was joined to an empire that ruled mainland China.

266
00:24:30.490 --> 00:24:37.020
In the centuries that followed, the memory of Koxinga became something different for everyone who looked back on him.

267
00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:47.710
The Qing dynasty, in time, came to embrace this man who had once been its enemy as a symbol of loyalty, and in the nineteenth century it allowed an official temple to be built in his honor.

268
00:24:47.710 --> 00:24:51.840
In Japan, it was never forgotten that his mother was Japanese.

269
00:24:51.840 --> 00:25:04.130
The great playwright Chikamatsu Monzaemon turned his life, in seventeen fifteen, into a puppet play whose title can be rendered as The Battles of Koxinga, and it became one of the best-loved works of the Japanese stage.

270
00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:10.180
In China, Koxinga is remembered as a national hero who drove a foreign colonizer into the sea.

271
00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:19.430
In Taiwan, he is counted among the island's true founders, and in the city of Tainan, a temple dedicated to him is still crowded with visitors today.

272
00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:24.040
For military historians, Zeelandia sheds light on another question.

273
00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:33.930
For a long time, it was common to explain Europe's expansion across the world in the seventeenth century by a decisive advantage in firearms, fortress design and sea power.

274
00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:37.580
Zeelandia shows that the picture is more complicated.

275
00:25:37.580 --> 00:25:40.750
The Dutch fortress truly was superior.

276
00:25:40.750 --> 00:25:45.200
It could not be taken by assault, and it held Koxinga back for months.

277
00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:49.170
Their ships, too, were genuinely powerful on the open sea.

278
00:25:49.170 --> 00:25:54.900
But on land, a disciplined, experienced army routed the Dutch musket.

279
00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:59.830
Knowledge of the shallow waters, the tides and the storms was on Koxinga's side.

280
00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:07.960
In the end, the fortress fell from a hill pointed out by a deserter, when knowledge taken from Europe was combined with the skill of Chinese artillery.

281
00:26:07.960 --> 00:26:13.850
Technology alone had not decided the victory; the experience of the hands that wielded it had.

282
00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:21.020
Today, in the Anping district of Tainan, you can visit a red-brick ruin, all that remains of the walls of Fort Zeelandia.

283
00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:27.870
Among the bricks stand old trees whose roots, over the centuries, have worked their way deep into the wall.

284
00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:33.920
This fortress, which once stood on the shore at the tip of a sandbar, now lies far from the sea.

285
00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:41.410
The lagoon where the ships of the Dutch and of Koxinga once sailed has silted up over time, giving way to the streets of the city.

286
00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:48.420
And on the site of Provintia, on the mainland side, there now rises a Chinese pavilion built in later years.

287
00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:54.790
Two structures that once turned their cannons on each other now stand side by side on the same city's tourist trail.

288
00:26:54.790 --> 00:26:59.080
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Tarih.

289
00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:02.410
Until we meet again in a new story.
